      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML certificate for water meters (R49).\n\nKey content:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\n- Product: QALCOSONIC W1 ultrasonic water meter\n- Section 2.3: Operation and presentation of legal data\n- Section 2.4: Software specification (table with software versions and checksums)\n- Section 2.5: Accountable alarms\n- Section 2.6: Integrated equipment and functions\n- Section 3: Technical and metrological data — Tab. 1 (DN15), Tab. 2 (DN20), Tab. 3 (starts but content not in this fragment)\n\nTable structure: The tables have a matrix structure with DN15 and DN20, each with multiple Q3 variants (1.6 and 2.5 m³/h for DN15; 2.5 and 4 for DN20), and within each Q3 there are multiple Q1 values corresponding to different ratio Q3/Q1 values.\n\nLet me work through Tab. 1 (DN15):\n- Q3 = 1.6 m³/h (5 columns), Q3 = 2.5 m³/h (5 columns)\n- Q1 values: 0.020, 0.010, 0.0064, 0.005, 0.004 | 0.031, 0.0156, 0.010, 0.0062, 0.0031\n- Q2 values: 0.032, 0.016, 0.010, 0.008, 0.0064 | 0.050, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010, 0.005\n- Q4: 2 | 3.125\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 80, 160, 250, 315, 400 | 80, 160, 250, 400, 800\n\nInteresting — for Q3=2.5, ratios listed are 80, 160, 250, 400, 800. Let's verify: Q3/Q1 = 2.5/0.031 = 80.6 ≈ 80; 2.5/0.0156 = 160.3 ≈ 160; 2.5/0.010 = 250; 2.5/0.0062 = 403 ≈ 400; 2.5/0.0031 = 806 ≈ 800. Yes.\n\nFor Q3=1.6: 1.6/0.020 = 80; 1.6/0.010 = 160; 1.6/0.0064 = 250; 1.6/0.005 = 320 (315); 1.6/0.004 = 400. Yes.\n\nSo there are 10 model variants in Tab.1 (DN15): combinations of (DN15, Q3, Q3/Q1 ratio).\n\nTab. 2 (DN20): 9 columns\n- Q3 = 2.5 (4 columns), Q3 = 4 (5 columns)\n- Q1: 0.031, 0.0156, 0.010, 0.0062 | 0.050, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010, 0.005\n- Q2: 0.05, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010 | 0.080, 0.040, 0.026, 0.016, 0.008\n- Q4: 3.125 | 5\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 80, 160, 250, 400 | 80, 160, 250, 400, 800\n\nVerify: 2.5/0.031=80; 2.5/0.0156=160; 2.5/0.010=250; 2.5/0.0062=403≈400. And 4/0.050=80; 4/0.025=160; 4/0.016=250; 4/0.010=400; 4/0.005=800. Yes, 9 variants for DN20.\n\nSo total 19 model variants across DN15 (10) and DN20 (9).\n\nAlso note that Tab. 3 is referenced (\"Tab. 3\" appears at the end) but its content is on the next page, not in this fragment. I should probably note only what's present.\n\nAlso, software table:\n- Version 1.03, checksum C983, remarks: DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\n- Version 1.03, checksum F897, remarks: DN15 B design, DN20 B design\n- Version 2.02, checksum CD00, remarks: DN40\n\nNote this mentions DN25, DN32, DN40 — those are probably in Tab. 3 (next fragment). The fragment includes the software table which mentions those DNs. Should I create models for DN25/DN32/DN40? They're mentioned in software remarks, so the cert covers them, but their metrological data is in Tab. 3 which isn't in this fragment. The instruction says \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\"\n\nHmm, this is a fragment of one certificate — I should extract what's here. The models from the Tab.1 and Tab.2 tables should be extracted. Should I include DN25/DN32/DN40 as models? They're mentioned only in the software remarks table. I could include them as model entries with just software attributes... That could be misleading. Better to include models only from the metrological tables (Tab 1, Tab 2) and record the software info separately. Actually, the software table's \"Remarks\" column ties software versions to DN sizes — this is important info. I could include it as a matrix_table.\n\nLet me decide: I'll build:\n\n- certificate: number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\", revision \"3\", scheme \"A\" (from pattern R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02). page_total 13. member_state: Slovakia (SLM = Slovak Legal Metrology). oiml_issuer_id: SK1? The certificate number contains \"SK1\" which likely is the issuer ID. But careful about fabricating. The stamp says \"SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY\". The cert number R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 suggests scheme A, issuer SK1. I think including member_state: \"Slovakia\" is reasonable given the stamp. oiml_issuer_id: \"SK1\" — reasonable inference from cert number. But rule 8: don't fabricate. The fragment only shows the cert number and stamp. Hmm, \"SK1\" in the number is strong evidence of issuer ID. I'll include it.\n\nActually, wait — this is a fragment (part 2 of 4), and parts 1, 3, 4 likely contain the header info. The instruction says to extract content present in this fragment only. The certificate number appears repeatedly. I'll include the certificate object with number, scheme, revision, page_total (13 from footer), member_state Slovakia.\n\n- certified_type:\n  - category: \"Water meter\" (R49 is water meters; the tables show water temperature range, meter temperature class, etc.)\n  - type_designations: [\"QALCOSONIC W1\"]\n  - description: brief description from sections 2.3-2.6? The fragment has operational data. I can add a description summarizing displays, software, alarms, integrated RF.\n\n- model_family:\n  - family_name: \"QALCOSONIC W1\" (the family name; models are per DN/Q3/ratio). Actually family name — the type designation QALCOSONIC W1 covers all. I'll set family_name \"QALCOSONIC W1\".\n  - models: model_id per variant. How to label? Something like \"DN15 Q3=1.6 R=80\" etc. Let me use format \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R80\". Hmm, be consistent and clear: \"DN15 Q3=1.6 m³/h (Q3/Q1=80)\". Keep simpler: model_id: \"DN15/Q3=1.6/R80\".\n\nActually let me think about what's cleanest for downstream use. The example in the rules: {\"model_id\": \"Emax=25t\"}. So they pack attributes into the ID. I'll do:\n\nDN15: \n1. DN15 Q3=1.6 R80 (Q1=0.020)\n2. DN15 Q3=1.6 R160 (Q1=0.010)\n3. DN15 Q3=1.6 R250 (Q1=0.0064)\n4. DN15 Q3=1.6 R315 (Q1=0.005)\n5. DN15 Q3=1.6 R400 (Q1=0.004)\n6. DN15 Q3=2.5 R80 (Q1=0.031)\n7. DN15 Q3=2.5 R160 (Q1=0.0156)\n8. DN15 Q3=2.5 R250 (Q1=0.010)\n9. DN15 Q3=2.5 R400 (Q1=0.0062)\n10. DN15 Q3=2.5 R800 (Q1=0.0031)\n\nDN20:\n11. DN20 Q3=2.5 R80\n12. DN20 Q3=2.5 R160\n13. DN20 Q3=2.5 R250\n14. DN20 Q3=2.5 R400\n15. DN20 Q3=4 R80\n16. DN20 Q3=4 R160\n17. DN20 Q3=4 R250\n18. DN20 Q3=4 R400\n19. DN20 Q3=4 R800\n\nModel ID format: \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R80\" — let me use hyphens: \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R80\".\n\nAttributes per model: nominal_diameter_dn (mm), q3 (m³/h), q1 (m³/h), q2 (m³/h), q4 (m³/h), ratio_q3_q1, ratio_q2_q1 (1.6 for all), connection_thread, construction_length_l (list of values), installation_position, water_temperature_range, meter_temperature_class, max_working_pressure_map (bar, 16), pressure_loss_delta_p (bar — varies by Q3), maximum_permitted_error_upper (±2/±3 %), maximum_permitted_error_lower (±5 %), scale_interval (m³, 0.001), scale_interval_testing (m³, 0.000001), capacity_calculator (m³ 999999.999), capacity_calculator_testing (m³ 999.999999), accuracy_class (2), mechanical_class (M1), climatic_class (°C -15 to +70), electromagnetic_class (E2), environmental_classification (B/O), flow_profile_sensitivity_class (U0D0), battery.\n\nSince attributes like q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio vary per model, they go in model_level. Constant attributes (dn within DN15 group varies though — dn varies between tables) go to model_level too where they vary; constant across all models → type_level.\n\nWhich attributes vary?\n- nominal_diameter_dn: 15 vs 20 → model_level\n- q3: varies → model_level\n- q1: varies → model_level\n- q2: varies → model_level\n- q4: varies → model_level\n- ratio_q3_q1: varies → model_level\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6 for all → type_level\n- connection_thread: G ¾ (DN15) vs G 1 (DN20) → varies → model_level\n- construction_length_l: DN15: 80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170; DN20: 105, 110, 130, 165, 190 → varies → model_level (value as list of numbers? Structured values require object shape. A list like \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\" — hmm. These are alternative construction lengths. Could represent as value being an array of numbers? The schema says value: number | {min,max} | string. A list isn't in there. I could store as string \"80/105/110/115/165/170\". Hmm. Or store as min/max range — no, they're discrete options. I'll keep as string of alternatives. Actually maybe I can put an array — schema says <number | {min, max} | string>. To conform strictly, use string \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\" with unit mm. That preserves the info.)\n- installation_position: Horizontal / Vertical → constant → type_level (string value)\n- water_temperature_range: multiple options: 0.1 to 30, 0.1 to 50, 30 to 70, 0.1 to 70 (note \"0,1 to.70\" is OCR typo for 0.1 to 70). These map to temperature classes T30, T50, T30/70, T70. This is config-level: the meter is available in 4 temperature classes; water temp range depends on class. Could put in config_level with axis \"meter_temperature_class\". Values: {condition: \"T30\", value: {min: 0.1, max: 30}}, etc. Also note decimal conversion: \"0,1\" → 0.1.\n- meter_temperature_class: T30/T50/T30/70/T70 — options; as type_level it's a list of alternatives. Hmm — it's a configuration axis. I could add config_level attribute meter_temperature_class with conditions... no, the class itself is the axis. Maybe better: config_level entry attribute \"water_temperature_range\", axis \"meter_temperature_class\". And leave meter_temperature_class out or as a type_level string list \"T30, T50, T30/70, T70\". I'll do config-level for water_temperature_range and also record meter_temperature_class as type_level with value string listing options? Rule 2 says lists of values → model matrix, but this is a config alternative list, not per-model. The config_level representation captures it. I'll include a type_level \"available_meter_temperature_classes\" maybe not — avoid fabrication of labels? It's extraction; labels need to be canonical snake_case. I can add it. Hmm, keep it simpler: config_level with axis meter_temperature_class covering both temperature range; plus a type_level entry meter_temperature_class? I think I'll include meter_temperature_class options in config_level only (as the axis), and water_temperature_range values per condition.\n\nActually, let me also add \"water_temperature_range\" per class:\n- T30: 0.1 to 30 °C\n- T50: 0.1 to 50 °C\n- T30/70: 30 to 70 °C (per table: \"30 to 70\")\n- T70: 0.1 to 70 °C (OCR \"0,1 to.70\")\n\nHmm, the OCR shows four ranges: \"0,1 to 30 / 0,1 to 50 / 30 to 70 / 0,1 to.70\" and four classes \"T30 / T50 / T30/70 / T70\". The pairing is natural: T30→0.1-30, T50→0.1-50, T30/70→30-70 (i.e., from 30 to 70), T70→0.1-70. Good.\n\n- maximum_working_pressure_map: 16 bar → type_level\n- pressure_loss_delta_p: 0.16 bar for Q3=1.6 (DN15) / 0.25 for Q3=2.5 (DN15); Tab2: 0.16 for Q3=2.5 (DN20) / 0.25 for Q3=4 (DN20). So pressure loss depends on Q3: 0.16 when Q3≤2.5? No wait — DN15 Q3=2.5 has 0.25, DN20 Q3=2.5 has 0.16. Interesting. So it varies by (DN, Q3) combination: (15,1.6)→0.16, (15,2.5)→0.25, (20,2.5)→0.16, (20,4)→0.25. So model_level per model: pressure_loss class per model. That's fine — model_level with values per model.\n\n- maximum permissible error upper: ±2% at t ≤ 30°C, ±3% at t > 30°C → config_level axis \"water_temperature\" or type_level? It's constant across models. It's a conditional value → config_level with axis \"temperature\" conditions \"≤30 °C\" → 2, \">30 °C\" → 3, unit %. Note OCR \"Ø ≤ 30°C\" — Ø is a mis-OCR of temperature symbol (θ or t). I'll use \"≤ 30 °C\".\n\nHmm wait — config_level values shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Per schema: config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}. Unit — I'll include unit_symbol in each value as the rules allow (\"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\").\n\n- max permissible error lower: ±5 % → type_level (value 5, unit %). Sign: ± — store value 5 with note? I'll store as 5 and maybe unit \"%\". The ± could be captured as value \"+/-5\"? Rules: numbers not strings. I'll keep value 5, unit \"%\" — the attribute name maximum_permitted_error_lower implies MPE ±5%. Fine.\n\n- scale_interval: 0.001 m³ → type_level\n- scale_interval_testing_mode: 0.000001 m³ → type_level\n- capacity_of_calculator: 999999.999 m³ → type_level\n- capacity_of_calculator_testing_mode: 999.999999 m³ → type_level\n- accuracy_class: 2 → type_level (also belongs in recommendation.accuracy_classes? That's for the Recommendation — R49 accuracy classes are 1, 2. The table says Accuracy class 2. I'll include recommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, accuracy_classes [2]? The certificate number \"R49/2013\" suggests R49 edition 2013. Scheme A. Yes, I'll include recommendation {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [\"2\"]}. Hmm accuracy_classes as strings \"2\"? Schema example says accuracy_classes[]. I'll use [\"2\"].\n- mechanical_class: M1 → type_level\n- climatic_class: -15 to +70 °C → type_level (value {min:-15,max:70}, unit °C)\n- electromagnetic_class: E2 → type_level\n- environmental_classification: B/O → type_level\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: U0D0 → type_level\n- battery: \"li-battery 3.6 V, life time 16 years\" — split: battery_voltage: 3.6 V; battery_life_time: 16 years; battery_type: \"Li\". Or a single battery characteristic string. I'll split into battery_type \"lithium\" (string), battery_voltage 3.6 V, battery_service_life 16 years (unit \"years\"). Reasonable.\n\nMatrix tables: preserve Tab.1 and Tab.2 as matrix_tables, and the software table.\n\nmatrix_tables entries:\n\n1. name \"Tab. 1 – Technical and metrological data, DN15\" columns: [nominal_diameter, q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio_q3_q1, ratio_q2_q1, connection_thread, construction_length, installation_position, water_temperature_range, meter_temperature_class, maximum_working_pressure, pressure_loss, mpe_upper_range, mpe_lower_range, scale_interval, scale_interval_testing_mode, calculator_capacity, calculator_capacity_testing_mode, accuracy_class, mechanical_class, climatic_class, electromagnetic_class, environmental_classification, flow_profile_sensitivity_class, battery]. Rows: 10 rows, one per variant.\n\nHmm, that's big but faithful. Column units row exists in the table (second cell). I'll include column_units like the R137 example.\n\n2. Same for Tab. 2 DN20, 9 rows.\n\n3. Software specification table: name \"Software specification\", columns [software_version, checksum, remarks], rows: [{software_version: \"1.03\", checksum: \"C983\", remarks: \"DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\"}, {software_version: \"1.03\", checksum: \"F897\", remarks: \"DN15 B design, DN20 B design\"}, {software_version: \"2.02\", checksum: \"CD00\", remarks: \"DN40\"}].\n\nModel level characteristics: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, dn, pressure_loss. Also connection_thread and construction_length vary by DN — could include as model_level with string values.\n\nLet me define model ids. Use \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R80\" style. Let me list them with attributes:\n\nDN15 (Tab 1), Q3=1.6:\n- m1: R80: Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032, Q4=2\n- m2: R160: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q4=2\n- m3: R250: Q1=0.0064, Q2=0.010, Q4=2\n- m4: R315: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008, Q4=2\n- m5: R400: Q1=0.004, Q2=0.0064, Q4=2\nQ3=2.5:\n- m6: R80: Q1=0.031, Q2=0.050, Q4=3.125\n- m7: R160: Q1=0.0156, Q2=0.025, Q4=3.125\n- m8: R250: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q4=3.125\n- m9: R400: Q1=0.0062, Q2=0.010, Q4=3.125\n- m10: R800: Q1=0.0031, Q2=0.005, Q4=3.125\n\nDN20 (Tab 2), Q3=2.5:\n- m11: R80: Q1=0.031, Q2=0.05, Q4=3.125\n- m12: R160: Q1=0.0156, Q2=0.025, Q4=3.125\n- m13: R250: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q4=3.125\n- m14: R400: Q1=0.0062, Q2=0.010, Q4=3.125\nQ3=4:\n- m15: R80: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080, Q4=5\n- m16: R160: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q4=5\n- m17: R250: Q1=0.016, Q2=0.026, Q4=5\n- m18: R400: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q4=5\n- m19: R800: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008, Q4=5\n\nModel IDs: \"DN15 Q3=1.6 R80\"? I'll use e.g. \"DN15-Q3=1,6...\" no — decimal point: \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R80\".\n\nNow model_level entries:\n\n- nominal_diameter_dn: unit mm: values 15 (×10), 20 (×9)\n- permanent_flowrate_q3: m³/h per model\n- minimum_flowrate_q1: m³/h per model\n- transitional_flowrate_q2: m³/h per model\n- overload_flowrate_q4: m³/h per model\n- ratio_q3_q1: dimensionless per model\n- pressure_loss_delta_p: bar per model (0.16/0.25...)\n- connection_thread: string per model (G ¾ / G 1) — hmm, \"G ¾\" contains a fraction character. Keep as \"G 3/4\" and \"G 1\"? I'll use \"G ¾\" as-is or \"G3/4\". I'll write \"G 3/4\".\n- construction_length_l: string list per model (mm).\n\nThat's a lot of values but it's correct. Total 19 values per attribute.\n\nWait — should pressure_loss be 0.25 bar for DN15 Q3=2.5? Tab 1: Pressure loss ΔP: 0.16 (first 5 cols) | 0.25 (last 5 cols). Yes.\n\nFor DN20 Tab 2: 0.16 (first 4, Q3=2.5) | 0.25 (last 5, Q3=4). Yes.\n\nAlso \"Maximum working pressure MAP: 16 bar\" constant → type_level.\n\nMPE upper: \"± 2 (at Ø ≤ 30°C); ± 3 (at Ø > 30°C)\". config_level: attribute maximum_permitted_error_upper_flowrates, axis \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition: \"≤ 30 °C\", value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"> 30 °C\", value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"}]. Apply to Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4.\n\nMPE lower: ±5 % type_level.\n\nNow about \"0,1 to.70\" — OCR artifact, intended \"0,1 to 70\". I'll use min 0.1 max 70.\n\nSoftware section: I could record type_level characteristics: software_version? It varies by DN. It's captured in matrix_tables (software spec table). Good enough. Maybe also add type_level \"software_versions: 1.03, 2.02\"? Skip — matrix table covers it.\n\nIntegrated equipment: components? \"Integrated RF transceiver, Wireless M-Bus, LoRaA, NFC, Radio NB IoT\" — these are integrated communication options, plus MCUs: Renesas RF10WMGAFB MCU (128 KB) OR Renesas R5F111PJGFB MCU (256 KB). Could record components[]:\n- {role: \"integrated_rf_transceiver\", type_designations: [\"Wireless M-Bus\", \"LoRa\", \"NFC\", \"Radio NB IoT\"], alternatives: null}\n- {role: \"microcontroller\", type_designations: [\"Renesas RF10WMGAFB (128 KB)\", \"Renesas R5F111PJGFB (256 KB)\"], alternatives: \"OR\"}\n\nHmm — components are meant for sub-assemblies of the certified type. The MCU choice is an alternative. I think including this is reasonable and faithful. Alternatively put in characteristics config-level. I'll use components with alternatives \"OR\" for MCU.\n\nActually let me reconsider: components[] with role \"integrated RF transceiver\" listing the protocols as type_designations — protocols aren't really type designations. Maybe better: characteristics type_level \"integrated_communication_interfaces\": value string \"Wireless M-Bus, LoRa, NFC, Radio NB IoT\". And components for MCU with alternatives. Hmm.\n\nI'll do:\ncomponents: [\n  {role: \"integrated_rf_transceiver\", type_designations: [\"Wireless M-Bus\", \"LoRa\", \"NFC\", \"Radio NB IoT\"], alternatives: null, characteristics: {}},\n  {role: \"microcontroller\", type_designations: [\"Renesas RF10WMGAFB\", \"Renesas R5F111PJGFB\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {flash_memory: ...}}\n]\n\nHmm, characteristics inside components — sub-characteristics per MCU differ (128 KB vs 256 KB). Simpler: type_designations: [\"Renesas RF10WMGAFB MCU (128 KB)\", \"Renesas R5F111PJGFB MCU (256 KB)\"], alternatives: \"OR\". Keep memory in the designation string. Fine.\n\nSection 2.3: displays — description. I'll fold into certified_type.description or omit. The description field: \"free-text description if present\". The fragment doesn't have a \"Description\" section per se; sections 2.3-2.6 describe functionality. I'll compose a brief description: \"Electronic water meter with calculator and indicating device; displays total measured volume (m³) and flow rate (m³/h); additional displays via configuration program \"W1 TOOL\"; accountable alarms on fault conditions; integrated RF transceiver (Wireless M-Bus, LoRa, NFC, NB IoT).\" That's a fair summary. Or keep description more minimal. I'll include a concise one.\n\nFootnotes: none present. revision_history: certificate shows \"Rev. 3\" — that's the current revision, not history. The footer \"V04/24-12-02\" is a document code. I won't create revision_history since no changes list present. But maybe record certificate.revision? Schema certificate doesn't have revision field... fields: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. I could put the full number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\" in number, or number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" and note revision elsewhere. Rule for revision_history says revision as string. I'll set number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" and add certificate revision? Not in schema. Hmm. I could include number as full \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\"? The schema example for revision_history has revision field. Since this is fragment 2 of 4, other parts handle header. I'll include number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" and revision_history: [{revision: \"3\", date: null, changes: null}]? Fabricating an entry with nulls is eh. Actually the \"Rev. 3\" indicates the current certificate revision — including revision_history with revision \"3\" and no date/changes is honest. But schema fields revision, date, changes — omitting date/changes keys is allowed by rule 8 (omit fields not present). I'll include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"3\"}]. Hmm, borderline. I'll include it since Rev. 3 is explicitly present.\n\npage_total: 13 (from \"Page 4 of 13 pages\"). member_state: \"Slovakia\". oiml_issuer_id: \"SK1\" from cert number — reasonable. scheme: \"A\".\n\nrecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [\"2\"]. R49 is \"Meters intended for the metering of water in closed conduits\" — actually edition: cert number says R49/2013, so edition 2013. accuracy_classes: from table, accuracy class 2. I'll include [\"2\"].\n\ntest_reports: not present in fragment. Omit.\n\nNow, units — unit_id: leave null everywhere (rule says leave null unless certain). Actually I'm fairly certain of some unitsdb IDs like \"u:degree_Celsius\"... but the rule says leave null unless certain. I'll leave null for all to be safe.\n\nDecimal conversions: 1,6 → 1.6; 2,5 → 2.5; 0,020 → 0.02; 0,010 → 0.01; 0,0064 → 0.0064; 0,005 → 0.005; 0,004 → 0.004; 0,031 → 0.031; 0,0156 → 0.0156; 0,0062 → 0.0062; 0,0031 → 0.0031; 0,032 → 0.032; 0,016 → 0.016; 0,008 → 0.008; 0,064 → wait Q2 col5 = 0,0064 → 0.0064. Tab2 Q2: 0,05; 0,025; 0,016; 0,010; 0,080; 0,040; 0,026; 0,016; 0,008 → 0.05, 0.025, 0.016, 0.01, 0.08, 0.04, 0.026, 0.016, 0.008. Q4: 2 → 2; 3,125 → 3.125; 5. Pressure loss 0,16 → 0.16; 0,25 → 0.25. Scale interval 0,001 → 0.001; testing 0,000001 → 0.000001 (1e-6). Calculator capacity 999999,999 → 999999.999; testing 999,999999 → 999.999999. Ratio Q2/Q1 1,6 → 1.6. Climatic -15 to +70 → {min:-15, max:70}.\n\nNote JSON number 0.000001 is fine. 999999.999 fine.\n\nNow build matrix_tables rows.\n\nTab.1 columns: [\"model\", \"nominal_diameter_dn\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"connection_thread\", \"construction_length_l\", \"installation_position\", \"water_temperature_range\", \"meter_temperature_class\", \"maximum_working_pressure\", \"pressure_loss\", \"mpe_upper_flowrates\", \"mpe_lower_flowrates\", \"scale_interval\", \"scale_interval_testing_mode\", \"calculator_capacity\", \"calculator_capacity_testing_mode\", \"accuracy_class\", \"mechanical_class\", \"climatic_class\", \"electromagnetic_class\", \"environmental_classification\", \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", \"battery\"]\n\ncolumn_units: [null, \"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null, null, \"mm\", null, \"°C\", null, \"bar\", \"bar\", \"%\", \"%\", \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\", null, null, \"°C\", null, null, null, null]\n\nRows for Tab.1 (10 rows): each row with values. water_temperature_range: list \"0.1 to 30 / 0.1 to 50 / 30 to 70 / 0.1 to 70\" or combined with class. I'll write \"0.1–30 (T30), 0.1–50 (T50), 30–70 (T30/70), 0.1–70 (T70)\"? That's editorializing. Simpler: water_temperature_range: \"0.1 to 30 / 0.1 to 50 / 30 to 70 / 0.1 to 70\", meter_temperature_class: \"T30 / T50 / T30/70 / T70\". Fine.\n\nmpe_upper: \"±2 (T≤30 °C) / ±3 (T>30 °C)\".\n\nbattery: \"Li battery 3.6 V, 16 years life\".\n\nThis is getting verbose but preserves fidelity. Rows are objects.\n\nTab.1 rows:\n\n1. {model: \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R80\", q3: 1.6, q1: 0.020, q2: 0.032, q4: 2, ratio_q3_q1: 80, ...pressure_loss: 0.16}\n2. DN15-Q3=1.6-R160: q1 0.010, q2 0.016, r 160, pl 0.16\n3. DN15-Q3=1.6-R250: q1 0.0064, q2 0.010, r250, 0.16\n4. DN15-Q3=1.6-R315: q1 0.005, q2 0.008, r315, 0.16\n5. DN15-Q3=1.6-R400: q1 0.004, q2 0.0064, r400, 0.16\n6. DN15-Q3=2.5-R80: q1 0.031, q2 0.050, r80, q4 3.125, pl 0.25\n7. DN15-Q3=2.5-R160: q1 0.0156, q2 0.025, r160, 0.25\n8. DN15-Q3=2.5-R250: q1 0.010, q2 0.016, r250, 0.25\n9. DN15-Q3=2.5-R400: q1 0.0062, q2 0.010, r400, 0.25\n10. DN15-Q3=2.5-R800: q1 0.0031, q2 0.005, r800, 0.25\n\nTab.2 rows (9):\n11. DN20-Q3=2.5-R80: q1 0.031, q2 0.05, q4 3.125, pl 0.16, thread G1, lengths 105/110/130/165/190\n12. DN20-Q3=2.5-R160: 0.0156/0.025\n13. DN20-Q3=2.5-R250: 0.010/0.016\n14. DN20-Q3=2.5-R400: 0.0062/0.010\n15. DN20-Q3=4-R80: q1 0.050, q2 0.080, q4 5, pl 0.25\n16. DN20-Q3=4-R160: 0.025/0.040\n17. DN20-Q3=4-R250: 0.016/0.026\n18. DN20-Q3=4-R400: 0.010/0.016\n19. DN20-Q3=4-R800: 0.005/0.008\n\nCommon columns in rows: dn, ratio_q2_q1 1.6, connection thread, construction length, installation position, water temp range, temp class, MAP 16, MPEs, scale intervals, capacities, classes, battery.\n\nShould I duplicate all constant columns in each row? The matrix_tables should preserve the table; including all columns in each row is faithful. But it's long. Alternatively rows only contain model-specific + note constants in description. The R137 example included constant columns (cyclic_volume 0.9 in each row). I'll include all columns for fidelity, but it makes the JSON huge. Given 19 rows × ~28 columns... that's a lot but acceptable? Hmm. It might be better to keep rows to the varying attributes and put shared attributes in description or rely on model_level/type_level. The matrix_tables purpose: \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". Since I AM populating model_level and type_level with everything, the matrix table is partially redundant. I could keep matrix rows slim: model, q1, q2, q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss — the truly varying columns — and describe the rest.\n\nLet me think about output size. 19 rows × 10 fields is fine. 19 × 28 fields with long strings is very large. I'll go middle ground: include the columns that vary across rows plus key constants compactly. Actually, let me include the varying ones only (model, nominal_diameter_dn, q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss) and put common attributes in the table \"description\". Wait, nominal_diameter is constant within each table but differs between tables — include it. q3 varies within table — include.\n\nPlan matrix_tables:\n\nTable \"Tab. 1 – DN15\": columns [model, nominal_diameter_dn, permanent_flowrate_q3, minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, overload_flowrate_q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss_delta_p], column_units [null,\"mm\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",null,\"bar\"], rows: 10 rows with those fields. description: \"Common to all rows: ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6; connection thread G ¾; construction length 80/105/110/115/165/170 mm; installation horizontal/vertical; water temperature ranges 0.1–30 (T30), 0.1–50 (T50), 30–70 (T30/70), 0.1–70 (T70) °C; MAP 16 bar; MPE upper ±2% (T≤30 °C), ±3% (T>30 °C); MPE lower ±5%; scale interval 0.001 m³ (testing 0.000001 m³); calculator capacity 999999.999 m³ (testing 999.999999 m³); accuracy class 2; mechanical class M1; climatic class −15/+70 °C; electromagnetic class E2; environmental class B/O; flow profile sensitivity U0D0; Li battery 3.6 V, 16 years.\"\n\nTable \"Tab. 2 – DN20\": same columns, 9 rows, description similar with G 1 and lengths 105/110/130/165/190.\n\nTable \"Software specification\": columns [software_version, checksum, applicable_models], rows 3.\n\nAlso model_level characteristics. Which attributes to include in model_level: nominal_diameter_dn, permanent_flowrate_q3, minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, overload_flowrate_q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss_delta_p, connection_thread, construction_length_l. Hmm — connection_thread and construction_length vary only by DN (2 values across 19 models). Could be config-level with axis nominal_diameter? Or type-level? They vary by model → model_level strictly. But listing 19 entries of \"G ¾\"... acceptable. Alternatively config_level: attribute connection_thread, axis \"nominal_diameter\", values [{condition:\"DN15\", value:\"G 3/4\"},{condition:\"DN20\", value:\"G 1\"}]. Similarly construction_length. That's cleaner. Rule 5: config axes for attribute × config-axis matrix. DN is arguably a model attribute, but using config_level for DN-dependent attributes is reasonable. Hmm, but DN itself is in model_level. Mixed approach might confuse. I'll put connection_thread and construction_length in model_level to be consistent (varying per model). Ugh, verbosity. Let me just do model_level for: dn, q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss_delta_p, connection_thread, construction_length_l. 9 attributes × 19 values. That's ~171 small objects. It's verbose but complete and correct.\n\nAlternatively trim: construction_length as config_level by DN (2 entries), connection_thread config_level by DN (2 entries). And keep model_level for the flow parameters (dn, q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio, pressure loss). Pressure loss varies by (dn, q3) combo — 4 distinct values across 19 models; model_level fine.\n\nActually pressure_loss: DN15-Q3=1.6→0.16, DN15-Q3=2.5→0.25, DN20-Q3=2.5→0.16, DN20-Q3=4→0.25. So model_level with 19 values it is. Or config_level with axis \"nominal_diameter + Q3\"? Messy. Keep model_level.\n\nLet me reconsider — maybe I reduce repetition: the schema allows model_level values[] with model and value. I'll write them all. It's mechanical but fine.\n\nActually, hold on. Should I include nominal_diameter in model_level? It varies (15/20). Yes include.\n\nWater temperature range config: config_level attribute \"water_temperature_range\", axis \"meter_temperature_class\", values:\n- {condition: \"T30\", value: {min: 0.1, max: 30}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- {condition: \"T50\", value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- {condition: \"T30/70\", value: {min: 30, max: 70}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- {condition: \"T70\", value: {min: 0.1, max: 70}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n\nAnd maybe type_level \"meter_temperature_classes\": value \"T30, T50, T30/70, T70\"? I'll include as type_level string \"T30 / T50 / T30/70 / T70\". Hmm — is that a \"value\"? It's a list of class options. I'll include it as a string; it's informative.\n\nActually wait, is \"T30/70\" pairing with \"30 to 70\"? T30/70 class means meter operates 30–70°C presumably for hot water. And T70 → 0.1 to 70. Given table order matches exactly (4 ranges, 4 classes), pairing by order is safe.\n\nMPE upper config: config_level attribute \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_flow_rates\", axis \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition: \"≤ 30 °C\", value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"> 30 °C\", value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"}].\n\nType level list — final:\n\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6, unit null\n- installation_position: \"Horizontal / Vertical\" (string)\n- maximum_working_pressure_map: 16, unit \"bar\"\n- maximum_permitted_error_lower_flow_rates: 5, unit \"%\" — hmm, the ± sign lost. Value could be \"+/-5\"? Rules: numbers not strings; 5 with unit %. I'll keep 5 and unit \"%\". The attribute name implies MPE (±). OK.\n- scale_interval: 0.001, \"m³\"\n- scale_interval_testing_mode: 0.000001, \"m³\"\n- calculator_capacity: 999999.999, \"m³\"\n- calculator_capacity_testing_mode: 999.999999, \"m³\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: {min:-15,max:70}, \"°C\" — climatic class in R49 is usually expressed as -15°C/+70°C, so range value with °C unit. Good.\n- electromagnetic_class: \"E2\"\n- environmental_classification: \"B/O\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- battery_type: \"Lithium\" — original \"li-battery\". I'll use \"Li\" string. Or skip and use combined. I'll include battery_voltage 3.6 \"V\", battery_service_life 16 \"years\", battery_type \"lithium\".\n- meter_temperature_class: \"T30 / T50 / T30/70 / T70\" (options)\n\nStructure for structured values: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}? The example shape shows {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. Rule 2's example includes footnote_markers. Do I need unit_id and footnote_markers keys on every value? The example in rule 2 shows them. I'll include unit_symbol always; unit_id null... that adds bulk. The schema yaml shows characteristics type_level: <label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}. So minimal: value, unit (or unit_symbol?), footnote_markers. Rule 2's strict shape: value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. I'll include value, unit_symbol, and footnote_markers only where needed (omit when empty? \"Empty strings are not values — use null or omit\" — I'll omit empty footnote_markers). unit_id: \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain\" — implies unit_id should be present as null. Including unit_id: null everywhere adds bulk; but the strict shape includes it. I'll include unit_id: null to conform. Hmm, 19×7 attributes × extra key... fine, JSON is cheap.\n\nActually, let me not include unit_id everywhere — rule says leave null unless certain; the caller looks up unit_symbol. Including \"unit_id\": null explicitly is safer for schema conformance. I'll include it. OK.\n\nNow the JSON. Let me draft.\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"Slovakia\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"SK1\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"QALCOSONIC W1\"],\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"QALCOSONIC W1\",\n    \"models\": [ 19 entries ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [...],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [\"2\"]\n  },\n  \"revision_history\": [{\"revision\": \"3\"}]\n}\n\nWait — accuracy_classes: should they be numbers or strings? R49 classes 1 and 2 are numeric accuracy classes. I'll use [\"2\"]? Hmm example in schema for R60: accuracy_classes[] e.g. [\"C\",\"D\"]? Not shown. I'll use [2]? The recommendation for R49 — class \"2\". I'll write [\"2\"] as strings to be safe? Actually numbers could be fine. The table \"Accuracy class: 2\". I'll use [\"2\"]... hmm. Let me use [2] numeric? For load cells classes are letters. For water meters, accuracy class is numeric (1, 2, 3). I'll go with [2]? String \"2\" preserves as-is. I'll use [\"2\"].\n\nHmm, also \"project_number\": not present. date_issued: not in fragment (footer \"V04/24-12-02\" is a form code, not the issue date necessarily). Omit.\n\nModule designation: water meter with integrated calculator/indicator — \"Not applicable\"? For R49 the module concept isn't like MID. I'll omit or null. I'll set null.\n\nComponents — let me finalize:\n[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"integrated_rf_transceiver\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"Wireless M-Bus\", \"LoRa\", \"NFC\", \"Radio NB IoT\"],\n    \"alternatives\": null\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"microcontroller\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"Renesas RF10WMGAFB MCU (128 KB)\", \"Renesas R5F111PJGFB MCU (256 KB)\"],\n    \"alternatives\": \"OR\"\n  }\n]\n\nHmm, the RF transceiver options — are they alternatives or all present? Text: \"Integrated RF transceiver, Wireless M-Bus, LoRa, NFC, Radio NB IoT\" — reads like a list of integrated communication interfaces (alternatives depending on variant). I'll keep as one component entry listing them, alternatives null. Fine.\n\nAlso software — add component? {role: \"software\", type_designations: [\"1.03 (C983)\", \"1.03 (F897)\", \"2.02 (CD00)\"]}? Covered by matrix table. Skip.\n\nDescription for certified_type: \"Electronic volumetric water meter (ultrasonic) with integrated calculator and indicating device. Legal data displayed: total measured volume (m³) and flow rate (m³/h); additional displays available via the configuration program \\\"W1 TOOL\\\" (forward/reverse flow volume, water temperature, date/time, status error code, accumulated volume, segment tests, user/control numbers, verification mode). The calculator and indication device automatically detect fault conditions during measurement and stop the measurement with an alarm indication (accountable alarms). No legally relevant data can be altered via the integrated RF transceiver (Wireless M-Bus, LoRa, NFC, NB IoT).\"\n\nCareful: \"ultrasonic\" — is that stated? QALCOSONIC name suggests ultrasonic but not explicitly stated in fragment. Don't fabricate — drop \"ultrasonic\". Category: \"Water meter\" — is that stated? The certificate is R49 (water meters), water temperature, meter temperature class... \"Water temperature range\" explicitly appears. R49 = water meters. I'll use \"Water meter\" — safe.\n\nNow the model_level arrays. Let me enumerate models with IDs:\n\nM1 \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R80\"\nM2 \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R160\"\nM3 \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R250\"\nM4 \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R315\"\nM5 \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R400\"\nM6 \"DN15-Q3=2.5-R80\"\nM7 \"DN15-Q3=2.5-R160\"\nM8 \"DN15-Q3=2.5-R250\"\nM9 \"DN15-Q3=2.5-R400\"\nM10 \"DN15-Q3=2.5-R800\"\nM11 \"DN20-Q3=2.5-R80\"\nM12 \"DN20-Q3=2.5-R160\"\nM13 \"DN20-Q3=2.5-R250\"\nM14 \"DN20-Q3=2.5-R400\"\nM15 \"DN20-Q3=4-R80\"\nM16 \"DN20-Q3=4-R160\"\nM17 \"DN20-Q3=4-R250\"\nM18 \"DN20-Q3=4-R400\"\nM19 \"DN20-Q3=4-R800\"\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n\n1. nominal_diameter_dn (mm): 15 (M1-M10), 20 (M11-M19)\n2. permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h): 1.6,1.6,1.6,1.6,1.6,2.5,2.5,2.5,2.5,2.5,2.5,2.5,2.5,2.5,4,4,4,4,4\n3. minimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h): 0.02,0.01,0.0064,0.005,0.004,0.031,0.0156,0.01,0.0062,0.0031,0.031,0.0156,0.01,0.0062,0.05,0.025,0.016,0.01,0.005\n4. transitional_flowrate_q2 (m³/h): 0.032,0.016,0.01,0.008,0.0064,0.05,0.025,0.016,0.01,0.005,0.05,0.025,0.016,0.01,0.08,0.04,0.026,0.016,0.008\n5. overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h): 2×5, 3.125×9, 5×5\n6. ratio_q3_q1: 80,160,250,315,400,80,160,250,400,800,80,160,250,400,80,160,250,400,800\n7. pressure_loss_delta_p (bar): 0.16×5, 0.25×5, 0.16×4, 0.25×5\n\nOptional: connection_thread (G 3/4 ×10, G 1 ×9) and construction_length_l — put as config_level by DN to reduce bulk:\n\nconfig_level:\n- {attribute: \"connection_thread\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\", values: [{condition: \"DN15\", value: \"G 3/4\"}, {condition: \"DN20\", value: \"G 1\"}]}\n- {attribute: \"construction_length_l\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\", values: [{condition: \"DN15\", value: \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\", unit_symbol: \"mm\"}, {condition: \"DN20\", value: \"105, 110, 130, 165, 190\", unit_symbol: \"mm\"}]}\n- {attribute: \"water_temperature_range\", axis: \"meter_temperature_class\", values: [T30: 0.1–30, T50: 0.1–50, T30/70: 30–70, T70: 0.1–70, unit °C]}\n- {attribute: \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_flow_rates\", axis: \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition: \"≤ 30 °C\", value: 2, unit: \"%\"}, {condition: \"> 30 °C\", value: 3, unit: \"%\"}]}\n\nThat's clean.\n\nHmm, but DN is a model attribute, and connection_thread keyed on DN as config axis — acceptable.\n\nShould water_temperature_range and meter_temperature_class apply per-model? They're the same across models, so config/type-level fine.\n\nNow matrix_tables. I'll write Tab 1 and Tab 2 fully with all columns? Let me decide final: I'll include the full column set but keep row values compact. Actually, you know, given model_